---
import { listings, fmtDate, licLabel } from '../../lib/data';
const CHECKED = 'Nov 25, 2026';
const money = (n: number) => '$' + n.toLocaleString('en-US', { minimumFractionDigits: n % 1 ? 2 : 0, maximumFractionDigits: 2 });
const get = (slug: string) => listings.find(l => l.slug === slug);
const S = {
  hsCatalog: 'https://legal.hubspot.com/hubspot-product-and-services-catalog',
  hsSales: 'https://blog.hubspot.com/sales/hubspot-sales-hub-pricing',
  espo: 'https://www.espocrm.com/pricing/',
  espoReq: 'https://docs.espocrm.com/administration/server-configuration/',
  espoImport: 'https://docs.espocrm.com/administration/import/',
  twenty: 'https://twenty.com/pricing',
  twentyReq: 'https://docs.twenty.com/developers/self-host/capabilities/docker-compose',
  twentyLic: 'https://github.com/twentyhq/twenty/blob/main/LICENSE',
  frappe: 'https://frappe.io/crm/pricing',
  krayin: 'https://github.com/krayin/laravel-crm#requirements',
  do: 'https://www.digitalocean.com/pricing/droplets',
  hubEspo: 'https://hub.docker.com/r/espocrm/espocrm/tags',
  hubTwenty: 'https://hub.docker.com/r/twentycrm/twenty/tags',
  hubKrayin: 'https://hub.docker.com/r/webkul/krayin/tags',
};
// Read Nov 25, 2026, ~1 AM CT, USD.
// HubSpot product catalog (legal.hubspot.com): Starter Core Seat $20/mo per seat; Professional Core Seat $50, Sales Seat $100;
//   Enterprise Core $75, Sales Seat $150. 500 / 3,000 / 5,000 included credits. (hubspot.com/pricing pages would not load from my box.)
// HubSpot Sales Hub pricing guide (blog.hubspot.com, updated 09/09/26): Free tools 2 seats $0; Starter $9 annual / $15 monthly;
//   Professional $90 annual / $100 monthly + $1,500 one-time onboarding; Enterprise $150 + $3,500 onboarding.
//   The two HubSpot sources disagree on Starter ($20 list in the catalog vs $15 monthly in the blog), so the table shows the catalog list price.
// EspoCRM Cloud: Basic $15/user/mo (min 3 users), Enterprise $25 (min 5), Ultimate $69 (min 10); daily backups kept 7 days.
// EspoCRM server docs: PHP 8.3-8.5, MySQL 8+/MariaDB 10.6+/PostgreSQL 15, php memory_limit 256M recommended; Docker setup offered.
// Twenty: Pro $9/user/mo, Organization $39, billed yearly (toggle shows yearly -25%); self-hosting the core is free; >= 2 GB RAM for Docker Compose.
//   License: mostly AGPLv3, files marked @license Enterprise under a commercial license, some packages MIT.
// Frappe CRM pricing: billed by site size on Frappe Cloud, unlimited users: Starter 0.5 vCPU/2 GB $20/mo, Standard 1 vCPU/4 GB $40/mo; AGPL-3.0 self-host free.
// Krayin README requirements: RAM 3 GB or higher, PHP 8.2+, MySQL 8.0.32+.
// DigitalOcean Basic: 2 GiB $12, 4 GiB $24; weekly backups +20%.
// Docker Hub amd64 compressed: espocrm/espocrm 10.1.4-apache (Nov 20) 260.1 MB; twentycrm/twenty v2.63.0 (Nov 24) 249.9 MB;
//   webkul/krayin 2.2.0 (Mar 20, 2026) 767.7 MB, 2.1.5 (Nov 10, 2025) 2,037.4 MB. No image under frappe/crm on Docker Hub.
const users = 5;
const small = 12 * 1.2, mid = 24 * 1.2;
const rows = [
  { slug: '', name: 'HubSpot Sales Hub Professional', best: 'Reference point: sequences, workflows, forecasting', cost: money(100 * users) + ' + ' + money(1500) + ' onboarding', lic: 'Proprietary' },
  { slug: '', name: 'HubSpot Starter (core seats)', best: 'Reference point: the entry paid plan', cost: money(20 * users) + ' list', lic: 'Proprietary' },
  { slug: '', name: 'HubSpot free tools', best: 'Contacts and deals for 2 users', cost: '$0 (2 seats only)', lic: 'Proprietary' },
  { slug: 'espocrm--espocrm', name: 'EspoCRM, self-hosted', best: 'Closest all-round sales CRM on a small server', cost: money(small) + ' server', lic: null },
  { slug: 'espocrm--espocrm', name: 'EspoCRM Cloud Enterprise', best: 'Same, hosted by the vendor', cost: money(25 * users), lic: null },
  { slug: 'twentyhq--twenty', name: 'Twenty, self-hosted', best: 'Modern UI, custom objects, API-first', cost: money(mid) + ' server', lic: 'AGPL-3.0 (mostly)' },
  { slug: 'twentyhq--twenty', name: 'Twenty Cloud Pro', best: 'Same, hosted, billed yearly', cost: money(9 * users), lic: 'AGPL-3.0 (mostly)' },
  { slug: 'frappe--crm', name: 'Frappe CRM on Frappe Cloud', best: 'Flat price, unlimited users; pairs with ERPNext', cost: money(40) + ' (Standard site)', lic: null },
  { slug: 'krayin--laravel-crm', name: 'Krayin CRM, self-hosted', best: 'MIT license, Laravel developers', cost: money(mid) + ' server', lic: null },
].map(r => ({ ...r, l: r.slug ? get(r.slug) : undefined }));
const fetched = rows.find(r => r.l)?.l?.fetchedAt;
const images = [
  { label: 'Twenty v2.63.0 (app only; also needs Postgres and Redis)', v: 249.9 },
  { label: 'EspoCRM 10.1.4 (Apache)', v: 260.1 },
  { label: 'Krayin 2.2.0', v: 767.7 },
  { label: 'Krayin 2.1.5', v: 2037.4 },
];
const maxI = 2040;
const faq = [
  { q: 'Is there a free open-source alternative to HubSpot CRM?', a: 'Yes. EspoCRM (AGPL-3.0), Frappe CRM (AGPL-3.0), Krayin (MIT) and Twenty (mostly AGPL-3.0) are all free to self-host with no user limit. You pay for the server, which for a small team is roughly ' + money(small) + ' to ' + money(mid) + ' a month on DigitalOcean with backups. HubSpot’s own free tools are also $0, but only for 2 seats.' },
  { q: 'How much does HubSpot cost for a small sales team?', a: 'Read ' + CHECKED + ' in HubSpot’s product catalog: a Starter core seat lists at $20 a month, and a Sales Hub Professional sales seat at $100. HubSpot’s own pricing guide adds a one-time $1,500 onboarding fee for Sales Hub Professional and says annual billing brings the seat to $90. For 5 reps that is ' + money(500) + ' a month on Professional plus ' + money(1500) + ' up front.' },
  { q: 'Does any of these replace HubSpot Marketing Hub?', a: 'Not really. These are sales CRMs: contacts, companies, deals, pipelines, tasks and email sync. For HubSpot-style marketing automation (lead scoring, campaigns, landing pages), the open-source option is Mautic, which I cover in the email marketing comparison. Plan on two tools where HubSpot is one.' },
  { q: 'Can I import my HubSpot data?', a: 'Through CSV, yes. Export contacts, companies and deals from HubSpot as CSV files, then use EspoCRM’s import tool, which maps CSV columns to fields, or Twenty’s CSV import (Twenty’s pricing page also points to its API for more than 50,000 records). I haven’t found a one-click HubSpot importer in any of them, and I haven’t run a HubSpot export through one myself yet. Emails, call logs and workflows won’t come across.' },
  { q: 'EspoCRM or Twenty?', a: 'EspoCRM is the safer pick for a classic sales team: it has been around for years, runs on plain PHP and MySQL, and has quotes, invoices, workflows and BPMN processes. Twenty is newer and nicer to use, with custom objects and a REST and GraphQL API, and it’s growing fast on GitHub. Pick Twenty if you have someone technical who wants to build on it; pick EspoCRM if you want a finished CRM tomorrow.' },
  { q: 'What server do I need to self-host a CRM?', a: 'It depends on the stack. Twenty’s docs ask for at least 2 GB of RAM, and it runs Postgres, Redis, a server and a worker, so I would start on 4 GiB. Krayin’s README asks for 3 GB of RAM or more, which also means a 4 GiB server. EspoCRM is a PHP app with a 256 MB memory_limit recommendation; for 5 users I’d try a 2 GiB server, which is my estimate rather than a vendor number.' },
];
const schema = { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'FAQPage', mainEntity: faq.map(f => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })) };
---
<section style="padding-top:6px"><div class="wrap prose">
<p class="note"><b>Checked on {CHECKED}.</b> GitHub numbers refresh nightly{fetched && <> (last fetch {fmtDate(fetched)})</>}. Prices are from each vendor’s page that night, in US dollars, before tax. HubSpot’s pricing pages wouldn’t load for me, so HubSpot figures come from its <a href={S.hsCatalog} rel="nofollow">product catalog</a> and its own <a href={S.hsSales} rel="nofollow">Sales Hub pricing guide</a>. <b>Disclosure:</b> no affiliate links on this page. HubSpot, EspoCRM, Twenty, Frappe, Krayin and DigitalOcean don’t pay us. <a href="/disclosure/">How this site makes money</a>.</p>

<h2>The short version</h2>
<ul>
  <li><b>You want a complete sales CRM you can run yourself today:</b> <a href="/tool/espocrm--espocrm/">EspoCRM</a>. Leads, deals, pipelines, quotes and workflows, on a small PHP server.</li>
  <li><b>You have a developer and want a modern CRM to build on:</b> <a href="/tool/twentyhq--twenty/">Twenty</a>, self-hosted or {money(9)} a user on their cloud.</li>
  <li><b>Your team keeps growing and per-seat pricing hurts:</b> <a href="/tool/frappe--crm/">Frappe CRM</a>. Its hosted plans are priced by server size with unlimited users.</li>
  <li><b>You use HubSpot mainly for marketing emails and landing pages:</b> none of these. Look at Mautic in the <a href="/compare/mailchimp-vs-mailerlite-vs-listmonk-vs-mautic/">email marketing comparison</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What 5 users cost per month</h2>
</div>
<div class="wrap"><div class="compare-wrap"><table class="compare">
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Option</th><th scope="col">Best for</th><th scope="col">5 users</th><th scope="col">License</th><th scope="col">Last push</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
{rows.map(r => <tr>
  <th scope="row">{r.l ? <a href={'/tool/' + r.slug + '/'}>{r.name}</a> : r.name}</th>
  <td>{r.best}</td>
  <td><span class="usd">{r.cost}</span></td>
  <td>{r.lic ?? (r.l ? licLabel(r.l.license) : '?')}</td>
  <td>{r.l ? fmtDate(r.l.lastPush) : 'n/a'}</td>
</tr>)}
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="note">Read {CHECKED}. <a href={S.hsCatalog} rel="nofollow">HubSpot catalog</a>: Starter core seat $20, Professional core seat $50, Sales Seat $100 (Professional) and $150 (Enterprise), per seat a month. HubSpot’s <a href={S.hsSales} rel="nofollow">pricing guide</a> lists Starter at $15 monthly or $9 a seat billed yearly, Professional at $100 monthly or $90 yearly plus a $1,500 onboarding fee, and 2 free seats; the two HubSpot sources don’t agree on Starter, so the table uses the catalog list price. <a href={S.espo} rel="nofollow">EspoCRM Cloud</a>: Basic $15 (3-user minimum), Enterprise $25 (5-user minimum), Ultimate $69 (10-user minimum), per user a month. <a href={S.twenty} rel="nofollow">Twenty</a>: Pro $9 and Organization $39 per user a month, billed yearly. <a href={S.frappe} rel="nofollow">Frappe CRM</a>: Starter site $20 (2 GB), Standard $40 (4 GB), unlimited users. Server rows are DigitalOcean Basic Droplets plus 20% for weekly backups (<a href={S.do} rel="nofollow">prices</a>): 4 GiB ($24) for Twenty and Krayin, whose docs ask for at least <a href={S.twentyReq} rel="nofollow">2 GB</a> and <a href={S.krayin} rel="nofollow">3 GB</a>; 2 GiB ($12) for EspoCRM, which is my estimate.</p>
</div>

<div class="wrap prose">
<h2>What I checked myself</h2>
<p>I didn’t install these tonight (my test box has no Docker), so I measured what I could: the size of each official Docker image, which is what you pull on every upgrade. Frappe CRM has no image under frappe/crm on Docker Hub; Frappe apps are built into a bench image with the frappe_docker setup, so there’s no like-for-like number.</p>
</div>
<div class="wrap"><div class="compare-wrap"><table class="compare">
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Image (amd64, compressed)</th><th scope="col">MB</th><th scope="col"><span class="muted small">Relative size</span></th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
{images.map(s => <tr><th scope="row">{s.label}</th><td>{s.v.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td><td><span style={'display:inline-block;height:10px;background:currentColor;opacity:.5;width:' + Math.max(2, Math.round(s.v / maxI * 220)) + 'px'}></span></td></tr>)}
</tbody></table></div></div>
<div class="wrap prose">
<p class="note">From Docker Hub tag pages, read {CHECKED}: <a href={S.hubEspo} rel="nofollow">espocrm/espocrm</a> (pushed Nov 20), <a href={S.hubTwenty} rel="nofollow">twentycrm/twenty</a> (pushed Nov 24) and <a href={S.hubKrayin} rel="nofollow">webkul/krayin</a>. The newest Krayin image is from March 2026, older than its latest code, and the one before it was over 2 GB, so the size swings a lot between releases.</p>

<h2>My picks, and why</h2>
<ul>
  <li><b>Best open-source HubSpot alternative for a sales team: EspoCRM.</b> It covers what most small teams use HubSpot’s Sales Hub for (leads, accounts, deals, pipelines, tasks, email) and adds quotes, invoices and BPMN workflows. It runs on PHP with MySQL, MariaDB or PostgreSQL, so almost any host can run it, and the vendor’s cloud is there if you don’t want to.</li>
  <li><b>Best to build on: Twenty.</b> The most-starred CRM I list on GitHub, with custom objects and a REST and GraphQL API. Know the license: most of it is AGPL-3.0, but files marked Enterprise are under a <a href={S.twentyLic} rel="nofollow">commercial license</a>.</li>
  <li><b>Best for a growing team on a budget: Frappe CRM.</b> Hosted pricing is per site, not per user, so 5 users and 25 users can cost the same {money(40)}. It also connects to ERPNext if you outgrow a CRM and need quotes and invoices in a full ERP.</li>
  <li><b>If your developers live in Laravel: Krayin.</b> MIT licensed, so there are no copyleft strings if you change it. It needs a bigger server than its feature list suggests.</li>
  <li><b>Smallest option:</b> <a href="/tool/alextselegidis--clientverse/">Clientverse</a>, a simple self-hosted CRM from the author of Easy!Appointments. It’s very young (a handful of GitHub stars), so I’d only use it for a one- or two-person shop.</li>
  <li><b>Stay on HubSpot if</b> marketing and sales share one database: forms, email campaigns, landing pages and attribution all feed the same contact record there. Replacing that takes two open-source tools and some glue.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Honest limits</h2>
<ul>
  <li>I haven’t run these with real sales data yet. Image sizes are measured; hardware figures are from the vendors’ docs, and the EspoCRM server size is my estimate.</li>
  <li>I didn’t test a HubSpot export into any of them. The import notes come from their docs.</li>
  <li>HubSpot’s live pricing pages wouldn’t load from my box, and HubSpot often runs Starter promotions, so check the price you’re actually offered.</li>
  <li>Server costs leave out a domain, email sending, backups beyond DigitalOcean’s weekly ones and your time on updates.</li>
</ul>

<h2>FAQ</h2>
{faq.map(x => <><h3>{x.q}</h3><p>{x.a}</p></>)}

